# Casbin / PermissionContext - gating rule (VCP)

Every authorization decision — in pages, components, shards, procedures,
**and** in `view!` markup — must go exclusively through Casbin via a
request-scoped `PermissionContext` (name may vary; the principle does not).

This portal is **not** the bastion. Do **not** copy
`../Vauban` policy CSV files, resource names, or BAC nests. Define a VCP
catalogue for org / license / billing / support surfaces, and keep
tenant isolation as a separate hard gate (see `portal-security.mdc`).

## In Topcoat pages and components

Topcoat prefers **functions over middlewares** for request-scoped concerns.
Load permissions once per request from `Cx` (memoized helper), then pass
or re-read that context — do not scatter ad-hoc Casbin `enforce` calls.

```rust
// Preferred shape (illustrative — wire to the real crate paths once scaffolded)
async fn require_perms(cx: &Cx) -> Result<PermissionContext> {
    // load role(s) + Casbin enforce for the VCP catalogue; fail closed
}

#[page("/org/members")]
async fn members(cx: &Cx) -> Result {
    let perms = require_perms(cx).await?;
    if !perms.members_read {
        return Err(/* 403 / redirect — fail closed */);
    }
    view! { /* ... */ }
}
```

- **Never** gate logic on `is_staff` / `is_superuser` / `is_admin` / raw
  role string compares.
- **Never** treat “logged in” as authorization for org-scoped data:
  always check the Casbin capability **and** the active organization /
  tenant boundary.
- Prefer a structural nest gate (layout or route helper that fails closed
  before any DB lookup) **plus** a handler / page body re-check for
  defence in depth — same pattern as the bastion BAC nests, applied to
  VCP admin / billing sub-trees when they exist.

## Permission catalogue (draft — refine at scaffold)

Map `PermissionContext` fields 1:1 to `(resource, action)` couples. A
starting catalogue for the customer portal (adjust names when product
IA is fixed):

| Resource       | Actions (examples)                                      |
|----------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| `profile`      | `read`, `write`                                         |
| `org`          | `read`, `write`, `manage`                               |
| `members`      | `read`, `invite`, `write`                               |
| `licenses`     | `read`, `download`, `manage`                            |
| `billing`      | `read`, `write`                                         |
| `support`      | `read`, `write`                                         |
| `admin`        | `view` (operator / internal surfaces, if any)           |

Adding a new field MUST come with:

1. A matching `p, role:..., r, a` line in the VCP policy source of truth
   (CSV / TOML / DB — pick one and keep it the only writer).
2. A tracked-permission registry used by tests (drift pin: every tracked
   permission is granted to at least one role).
3. Denial-path tests for the new gate (wrong role, wrong org, anonymous).

Until the policy file and loader exist, keep this rule as the target
shape; do not invent a second authorization style “temporarily”.

## In `view!` templates

Any conditional that shows an **actionable** control (edit, invite,
download license, manage billing) must read Casbin flags, not role
booleans:

```rust
// FORBIDDEN — short-circuits custom policies
if user.is_admin {
    button("Invite member")
}

// OK — delegates to Casbin via PermissionContext
if perms.members_invite {
    button("Invite member")
}
```

Role badges for display may still show a human-readable role label.
Display is not a gate.

## Forbidden patterns

| Forbidden | Required instead |
|-----------|------------------|
| `if user.is_admin` / `is_staff` / `is_superuser` as a gate | `if perms.<resource>_<action>` |
| Trusting a client-supplied org id without server check | Resolve active org from session + membership |
| Casbin check alone for object access | Casbin capability **and** tenant / ownership check |
| Copying bastion `assets:*` / `sessions:*` resources | VCP catalogue only |
| Silent success when `PermissionContext` is missing | Fail closed (403 / login redirect) |

## Two-layer authorization (Casbin + tenant / entitlement)

`PermissionContext` is **necessary but not sufficient**.

1. **Casbin / `PermissionContext`** — functional capability (“may this
   principal invite members / download licenses at all?”). Customizable
   via policy.
2. **Tenant + entitlement** — instance-level (“may they act on *this*
   organization / license / invoice?”). Enforce on every object id;
   anti-enumeration: prefer 404 over 403 when existence would leak
   across tenants.

Do not collapse these into a single flag. A handler that needs
“may caller X act on license Y?” checks **both**.

Hard product invariants that Casbin must not override (e.g. “org always
keeps at least one owner”, “cannot remove own last billing admin”)
belong in dedicated invariant helpers — same idea as bastion
`role_invariants`, different domain.

## Why

Hardcoding role booleans short-circuits custom policies and drifts from
server enforcement. A single `PermissionContext` keeps HTML, shards, and
procedures consistent with Casbin. Tenant checks prevent IDOR even when
a capability is broadly granted.
